# Global coding preferences

Never delete, empty, replace, rotate, regenerate, or redact an existing value in
any `.env` file unless the user explicitly requests that exact value change.
Preserve secrets and other configuration values even when the user has shared
them directly. Never remove a value merely because it is sensitive. Before any
authorized `.env` edit, preserve all unrelated keys and values exactly as they
are.

Do not add comments or docstrings to source code unless they are functionally
required for correctness, tooling, interoperability, or a non-obvious safety
constraint. Prefer clear names and structure over explanatory comments.

When a project provides a Docker Compose configuration, run backend commands,
tests, linters, formatters, migrations, and backend scripts inside the relevant
Docker Compose service rather than directly on the host.

Do not run end-to-end (E2E) tests. The user always runs E2E suites and reports
any failures that need to be diagnosed and fixed.

Never inspect, read, search, display, or load into the model context the contents
or diffs of dependency lockfiles, generated dependency artifacts, or local
environments. This includes `uv.lock`, `package-lock.json`, `npm-shrinkwrap.json`,
`pnpm-lock.yaml`, `yarn.lock`, `bun.lock`, `bun.lockb`, `node_modules`, `.venv`,
`venv`, `pyenv`, `__pycache__`, and equivalent lockfiles, generated artifacts,
dependency directories, or environment directories.

Always exclude these paths from `git diff`, repository-wide searches, file
listings, reviews, and similar commands, even when they appear as modified or
untracked files. Never load large generated files, caches, build outputs, binary
artifacts, logs, coverage output, or any other content unnecessary for the task
into the model context when it could consume substantial context.

Do not run frontend production builds, including `pnpm run build`. The user
always runs frontend builds and reports any failures that need to be diagnosed
and fixed.
